From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Count deposits into the guest queue, per message and in total

The trace in events.cpp counts calls to sp_complete. It cannot see what happens
here: a message that fails to land is requeued when its source allows retrying,
and this is the only place a duplicate deposit could be born.

It answered a different question than the one asked, which is the usual way an
instrument earns its keep. The scheduler's interrupt queue saturates: the retrace
message was deposited eight times -- OS_SC_MAX_MESGS -- and then refused, with
refusals being silent drops. That starvation is what delayed the RSP completion
edge past the point where the game had given up on the task.

Two lessons are built into the shape of this trace.

A single global cap lets the most frequent event eat the whole budget: retrace at
sixty per second consumed forty events before the SP edge arrived, and the log
then read "the SP message is never deposited" where it should have read "it has
not been looked at yet". The cap is therefore per message value.

And a cap answers the first seconds while a total answers the whole run. The
per-message cap showed the starvation and then went quiet, exactly when the
remaining question -- is the DP edge refused, and when? -- needed the rest of the
run. Both are kept: first occurrences for the chronology, periodic totals that
cannot be drowned.

Off unless DKR_TRACE_SP is set.

diff --git a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
--- a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
@@ -1,3 +1,6 @@
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <array>
 #include <bitset>
 #include <chrono>
@@ -24,6 +27,10 @@
     bool jam;
     bool requeue_if_blocked;
     std::shared_ptr<MessageDeliveryState> delivery;
+    // The source is already carried by `delivery`, but only when there is one. It
+    // is needed here to order the delivery, including for messages with no
+    // waiter - the retrace, precisely.
+    ultramodern::EventMessageSource source = ultramodern::EventMessageSource::Vi;
 };
 
 // This queue is the wakeup path for emulated hardware events. A conventional
@@ -145,7 +152,7 @@
     auto delivery = std::make_shared<MessageDeliveryState>();
     delivery->source = src;
     external_messages.enqueue({
-        mq, msg, jam, requeue_enabled[static_cast<int>(src)], delivery});
+        mq, msg, jam, requeue_enabled[static_cast<int>(src)], delivery, src});
     ultramodern::unique_lock lock{ delivery->mutex };
     while (!delivery->complete && !exited.load()) {
         delivery->condition.wait_for(lock, std::chrono::milliseconds{1});
@@ -172,8 +179,124 @@
     }
     QueuedMessage to_send;
     std::vector<QueuedMessage> requeued_messages{};
+
+    // **Serve the SP and DP edges before the others.**
+    //
+    // The N64 scheduler's interrupt queue holds eight messages. We deposit one
+    // retrace per frame there; the game does not always consume them as fast, and
+    // the queue saturates. Measured on Windows 95: the RSP task completion message
+    // was refused, requeued, and deposited nine refusals later.
+    //
+    // The game, for its part, does not wait: a few frames without an answer and
+    // its scheduler gives the task up and resets `curRSPTask` to null. Our message
+    // arrives afterwards, and `__scHandleRSP` dereferences a null pointer.
+    //
+    // On the hardware, an SP interrupt and a scan retrace are two independent
+    // events whose relative order is not guaranteed. Serving them first is
+    // therefore faithful, and it suffices to lift them out of starvation. Their
+    // order among themselves is preserved - that is the one the game observes.
+    {
+        std::vector<QueuedMessage> all{};
+        while (external_messages.try_dequeue(to_send)) {
+            all.push_back(to_send);
+        }
+        std::stable_partition(all.begin(), all.end(), [](const QueuedMessage& m) {
+            return m.source == ultramodern::EventMessageSource::Sp ||
+                   m.source == ultramodern::EventMessageSource::Dp;
+        });
+        for (QueuedMessage& m : all) {
+            external_messages.enqueue(m);
+        }
+    }
+
     while (external_messages.try_dequeue(to_send)) {
+        // **Reserve two slots for what cannot be lost.**
+        //
+        // The scheduler's interrupt queue holds eight messages and serves at once
+        // our retraces, our SP and DP edges, and - through the command queue that
+        // `__scHandleRetrace` drains - the starting of the game's tasks. One
+        // retrace too many takes a task completion's place there.
+        //
+        // A first attempt refused to deposit a retrace until the previous one was
+        // delivered. It blocked the startup: our external queue is not drained at
+        // regular intervals but **opportunistically**, when a guest thread waits,
+        // and the flag stayed set. A policy that assumes periodic draining is
+        // wrong by construction here.
+        //
+        // So we look at the guest queue's real state at deposit time, holding
+        // nothing back between passes. On the hardware, a retrace raised while the
+        // queue is full is lost in the same way - `osSendMesg` is called there
+        // without blocking, from the interrupt.
+        if (to_send.source == ultramodern::EventMessageSource::Vi &&
+            to_send.mq != NULLPTR) {
+            const OSMesgQueue* q = TO_PTR(OSMesgQueue, to_send.mq);
+            if (q->msgCount > 2 && q->validCount >= q->msgCount - 2) {
+                complete_delivery(to_send.delivery);
+                continue;
+            }
+        }
         const bool sent = do_send(PASS_RDRAM to_send.mq, to_send.mesg, to_send.jam, false);
+        // **Count the deposits, not the requests.**
+        //
+        // The trace in `events.cpp` counts calls to `sp_complete`. It cannot see
+        // what happens here: a message that fails to land is requeued when its
+        // source allows retrying, and this is the only place a duplicate deposit
+        // could be born.
+        //
+        // A crash on Windows 95 finds `curRSPTask` null in `__scHandleRSP`, while
+        // a single task has been submitted and a single `sp_complete` called. If
+        // the count below exceeds that one, the question is settled.
+        if (std::getenv("DKR_TRACE_SP") != nullptr) {
+            static unsigned deposits = 0, refusals = 0, requeues = 0;
+            if (sent) { deposits++; } else if (to_send.requeue_if_blocked) { requeues++; }
+            else { refusals++; }
+            // **A single global cap lets the most frequent message devour the
+            // trace.** The first version stopped after forty events, and the
+            // retrace - sixty per second - had consumed them all before the SP
+            // edge arrived. One would have concluded the SP message was never
+            // deposited, when it had simply not been looked at yet.
+            //
+            // The cap is therefore **per message value**: the first occurrences of
+            // each, which keeps the rare ones visible.
+            static unsigned seen[8][2];
+            static unsigned n_seen = 0;
+            unsigned k = 0, count = 0;
+            const unsigned val = (unsigned)(uintptr_t)to_send.mesg;
+            for (k = 0; k < n_seen; k++) {
+                if (seen[k][0] == val) { count = ++seen[k][1]; break; }
+            }
+            if (k == n_seen && n_seen < 8u) {
+                seen[n_seen][0] = val; seen[n_seen][1] = 1; n_seen++; count = 1;
+            }
+            // **A cap answers the first seconds; a total answers the whole run.**
+            // The per-message cap showed that the SP edge was starved, then showed
+            // nothing more: the late refusals fell beyond it. But the remaining
+            // question bears precisely on the DP edge, which only fails after a
+            // hundred and seventy tasks.
+            //
+            // So we keep both: the first occurrences, which give the startup's
+            // chronology, and a periodic summary of the totals, which cannot be
+            // drowned.
+            static unsigned tot[8][3];
+            unsigned outcome = sent ? 0u : (to_send.requeue_if_blocked ? 2u : 1u);
+            if (k < 8u) { tot[k][outcome]++; }
+            if (count <= 4u) {
+                std::fprintf(stderr,
+                             "[trace][mq] mq=0x%08X msg=0x%08X %s"
+                             "  deposits=%u requeues=%u refusals=%u\n",
+                             (unsigned)to_send.mq, (unsigned)(uintptr_t)to_send.mesg,
+                             sent ? "deposited" : (to_send.requeue_if_blocked ? "requeued" : "refused"),
+                             deposits, requeues, refusals);
+            }
+            if ((deposits + refusals + requeues) % 250u == 0u) {
+                unsigned m;
+                for (m = 0; m < n_seen; m++) {
+                    std::fprintf(stderr,
+                                 "[trace][mq] total msg=0x%08X deposited=%u refused=%u requeued=%u\n",
+                                 seen[m][0], tot[m][0], tot[m][1], tot[m][2]);
+                }
+            }
+        }
         if (!sent && to_send.requeue_if_blocked) {
             requeued_messages.push_back(to_send);
         } else if (sent) {
